Two things this file is NOT saying.

It is not a statement that the code will always be closed. Reserving every
right is the only starting position from which every later choice is still
available: a licence can always be made more permissive afterwards, and the
parts of this repository worth opening — the game engine most of all — may be
opened under their own terms when they are ready to stand on their own. The
reverse is not possible. Code released under an open licence stays released
under it for the copies already made, whatever this file says later.

It is not a claim over other people's work. The dependencies in package.json
are covered by their own licences and nothing here alters them. Where this
project has learned from published research on game engines, what it took was
the published method and not the source — the ideas in a paper are free to
anyone who reads them, and an engine's code is not.

Questions about use, or about a licence for any part of this, go to
hello@itsutsu.com.
